---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Fleet tracking demo | Zakaria Zerrouk"
  description="Case study: a simulated fleet and the logic that turns raw GPS positions into geofence alerts, trips, stops and reports."
>
  <article class="case mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 pb-24 pt-12">
    <p><a href="/#projects">← All projects</a></p>

    <h1 class="mt-6 font-display text-5xl font-extrabold uppercase leading-[0.95] tracking-tight md:text-6xl">Fleet tracking demo</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-muted">
      A simulated fleet, and the logic that turns raw GPS positions into
      geofence alerts, trips, stops and per-vehicle reports. It runs in the
      browser and as a Docker pipeline with MQTT and PostgreSQL. It is an
      independent project: original code and synthetic data, with no employer
      material.
    </p>
    <p class="font-mono text-sm">
      <a href={site.demo} rel="noopener">Live demo</a> ·
      <a href={site.demoRepo} rel="noopener">Source on GitHub</a>
    </p>

    <img
      src="/images/fleet-demo.png"
      alt="The demo: a map of Casablanca and Mohammedia with five truck and van icons, a list of geofence alerts on the left and a fleet report table underneath."
      width="1581"
      height="1207"
      loading="lazy"
      class="mt-6 h-auto w-full rounded-lg border border-border"
    />

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      A fleet operator does not need raw GPS points. They need to know when a
      vehicle left the depot, where it stopped and for how long, and how far it
      drove. Getting from positions to those answers means deciding what counts
      as a stop, when a vehicle has genuinely left a zone, and how to handle
      messages that arrive late or twice.
    </p>

    <h2>What it does</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Simulates five vehicles on looping routes around Casablanca and Mohammedia, with GPS noise.</li>
      <li>Raises an alert when a vehicle enters or leaves a circular zone (the depot).</li>
      <li>Detects stops and the trips between them.</li>
      <li>Totals trips, distance, driving time and stopped time for each vehicle.</li>
      <li>Shows the selected vehicle's trips, stops and geofence events as a timeline.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Architecture</h2>
    <pre>Browser demo:     simulator → detectors → map, alerts, report

Docker pipeline:  publisher → Mosquitto → ingest → PostgreSQL
                  (simulator)  (MQTT broker)  (validation + detectors)</pre>
    <p>
      All detection logic lives in a single TypeScript package of plain
      functions with no dependency on the browser, MQTT or the database. The
      browser demo and the ingest service call the same code, which is tested
      in isolation. The map uses MapLibre with OpenStreetMap tiles.
    </p>

    <h2>Decisions and trade-offs</h2>

    <h3>Stops are detected by dwell time, not speed</h3>
    <p>
      Speed derived from two GPS points is unreliable: a parked vehicle that
      drifts 5 m in a second appears to travel at 18 km/h. A stop is therefore
      defined as a vehicle staying within 50 m of one spot for three minutes.
      The trade-off is latency, since a stop can only be confirmed three
      minutes after it begins.
    </p>

    <h3>Trip times are retroactive</h3>
    <p>
      Because confirmation is delayed, a trip's end is recorded as the moment
      the vehicle arrived, not the moment the system noticed. Results arrive
      late but accurate, which means the report can lag the map by a few
      minutes.
    </p>

    <h3>Geofence exits use a buffer</h3>
    <p>
      GPS jitter at a zone boundary would otherwise produce a stream of false
      enter and exit events. A vehicle must travel 20 m beyond the edge before
      it counts as having left. The jitter test fails when the buffer is set to
      zero, which confirms that the buffer is what prevents the false events.
    </p>

    <h3>Late, duplicate and restarted streams</h3>
    <p>
      A position at or before the vehicle's last timestamp is dropped. A
      position more than an hour earlier is treated as a stream restart: the
      open trip or stop is saved and the vehicle's state is reset. The ingest
      service also rejects malformed JSON, out-of-range values and messages
      whose topic and vehicle id disagree.
    </p>

    <h3>Deliberate omissions</h3>
    <p>
      Raw positions are not stored. At the default speed that would exceed
      400,000 rows per day, and nothing in the project needs them, although it
      means history cannot be replayed. Kafka is not used, because a fleet of
      this size does not need it, and there is no ORM, because four small
      tables are well served by plain SQL.
    </p>

    <h3>Two ways to run it</h3>
    <p>
      The hosted demo runs the simulation and detectors in the browser, so it
      is free to host and has no infrastructure to maintain. The Docker version
      uses a real broker and database. The hosted demo therefore simulates the
      pipeline and does not use MQTT or PostgreSQL itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Evidence it works</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Over 50 unit tests cover the detection logic. Some expected values are
        derived by hand, for example a simulated 3 km route and the exact
        trips, stops and geofence events it must produce.
      </li>
      <li>
        The full pipeline was run in Docker and the stored PostgreSQL rows were
        checked against expected values. For example, one trip was stored as
        9,001 m, against a straight-line distance of about 9.0 km between its
        two points.
      </li>
      <li>
        The broker was restarted mid-run. The ingest service reconnected,
        resubscribed and continued saving.
      </li>
      <li>The <a href={site.demo} rel="noopener">live demo</a> is deployed and runs in the browser.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is a demo with synthetic data, not production software.</li>
      <li>Vehicles drive straight lines at constant speed, so on the map they sometimes cut across buildings.</li>
      <li>A trip can end up to about 50 m short of the point where the vehicle actually stopped.</li>
      <li>Detector state is held in memory, so restarting the ingest service loses any trip or stop in progress. A failed database write loses the results of that message.</li>
      <li>Delivery is at most once, there is a single ingest instance, and there is no authentication.</li>
      <li>The publisher and ingest services have no automated tests. They were checked by running the full pipeline.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The <a href={site.demoRepo} rel="noopener">README</a> lists these in full.
    </p>

    <h2>Next steps</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Route vehicles along real roads instead of straight lines.</li>
      <li>Support polygon zones as well as circles.</li>
      <li>Persist detector state so a restart does not lose work in progress.</li>
      <li>Add automated tests for the publisher and ingest services, and authentication on the broker.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>